  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(318.1pt, 134.84pt, "The universality of flexoelectricity is ")
  #line-at(90.02pt, 158.24pt, "guaranteed by the fourth rank nature of the flexoelectric tensor that couples strain ")
  #line-at(90.02pt, 181.64pt, "gradient to polarization: being even-parity, it is compatible with materials of ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(488.04pt, 181.64pt, "any")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(90.02pt, 205.04pt, "symmetry [")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#0066ff"))
  #line-at(146.06pt, 205.04pt, "10")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(158.06pt, 205.04pt, "]. Another consequence of even-parity is that flexoelectric polarization ")
  #line-at(90.02pt, 228.44pt, "should be insensitive to space inversion in all materials, including those with that ")
  #line-at(90.02pt, 251.84pt, "already have spontaneous polarization and thus a broken inversion symmetry. In other ")
  #line-at(90.02pt, 275.24pt, "words: flipping a ferroelectric crystal upside down should have no effect on its ")
  #line-at(90.02pt, 298.64pt, "flexoelectric coefficient.  ")
  #line-at(90.02pt, 345.44pt, "Mathematics being an exact science, this expectation is of course mathematically ")
  #line-at(90.02pt, 368.84pt, "correct. However, it rests on a physically incomplete picture -and is therefore ultimately ")
  #line-at(90.02pt, 392.24pt, "wrong. As we will show, the bending-induced polarization of poled ferroelectrics ")
  #line-at(90.02pt, 415.66pt, "cannot be adequately described by the linear flexoelectric tensor and includes a ")
  #line-at(90.02pt, 439.06pt, "nonlinear term at all curvatures -we have observed nonlinearity at curvatures as small ")
  #line-at(90.02pt, 462.46pt, "as 10")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 7.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(116pt, 457.96pt, "-2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
